ruleDoorT -- SEQ 554 (Tony, 2026-11-07): ITERATe compiled through the rule door. Rows prefixed RD-.

Not run alone: pop.sh builds each row's input, everything above this file's stop(); then the row, then a sentinel, one process per
arm under an alarm. The arms are interpreted (no opt-in) and compiled (jitterRule(ITERATe);). Every RD row is read by value, by exit
and by sentinel, on both arms, and compiled must equal interpreted AND the pinned value. A row whose value could be produced by the
interpreted road alone is why the per-fire count is asserted beside it: a door that never opened would agree with itself.

ATTRIBUTES / MEMBERS rows are the ones that matter: aMint := copyOf(aOn) is a handed-over read of a natively written local, and the
filter it carries decides what the loop walks. RD-6 and RD-7 fire twice in one process with different terms, so anything fixed at
the first fire (the compile) reads wrong on the second.

RG- rows drive three scratch rules, because ITERATe cannot reach the R2 guard on a parse: every path of it ends in a return. KEEPs
returns its label; REFUSEs refuses mid-body; FELLOFf runs off its end. it1 is preset to "untouched" so a fire that adopts something
shows it. FELLOFf is the one deliberate difference: interpreted adopts its last value (1), compiled refuses by name and adopts nothing.
The running-off and refusal behaviour itself is docketed, not cured (SEQ 554 R2, docs/ruleJitRecon.md).

